WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.515
Safe Rust protects buffers through
multiple reinforcing rules.

2
00:00:03.595 --> 00:00:07.048
Ownership clarifies who controls a value
and when that value is cleaned up.

3
00:00:07.128 --> 00:00:09.540
Borrowing controls who may read or mutate
the value.

4
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.867
Lifetimes help ensure references remain
valid.

5
00:00:11.947 --> 00:00:14.304
These rules work together before the
program runs.

6
00:00:14.384 --> 00:00:17.234
The standard buffer abstractions carry
structure with them.

7
00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:18.520
Arrays have fixed length.

8
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.683
`Vec<T>` owns a growable sequence of
values.

9
00:00:20.763 --> 00:00:22.627
Slices borrow views into existing data.

10
00:00:22.707 --> 00:00:24.187
`String` owns valid UTF-8 text.

11
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:25.802
`&str` borrows valid UTF-8 text.

12
00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:27.417
Byte slices represent raw bytes.

13
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:30.511
Each abstraction communicates different
ownership, validity,

14
00:00:30.591 --> 00:00:31.797
and access expectations.

15
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:33.138
Safe access checks bounds.

16
00:00:33.218 --> 00:00:35.739
If code uses safe indexing and an index is
out of bounds,

17
00:00:35.819 --> 00:00:39.601
Rust produces controlled failure behavior
instead of allowing arbitrary memory

18
00:00:39.681 --> 00:00:40.065
access.

19
00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:43.927
A panic is still an operational concern
because it can affect availability or user

20
00:00:44.007 --> 00:00:46.584
experience, but it is not the same as
memory corruption.

21
00:00:46.664 --> 00:00:49.240
That distinction is important during
security review.

22
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.896
A service should still avoid panics on
untrusted input,

23
00:00:51.976 --> 00:00:54.662
but a panic from safe indexing is a
contained failure mode.

24
00:00:54.742 --> 00:00:58.305
It does not mean another buffer was
overwritten or unrelated memory was read.

25
00:00:58.385 --> 00:01:02.112
Defensive code should design predictable
error handling rather than relying on

26
00:01:02.192 --> 00:01:03.672
panics as routine control flow.

27
00:01:03.752 --> 00:01:06.657
Safe abstractions should be the default
for application code.

28
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:10.026
If a design seems to require raw pointers
or unchecked buffer behavior,

29
00:01:10.106 --> 00:01:10.928
pause and ask why.

30
00:01:11.008 --> 00:01:13.859
Often a slice, iterator, checked access
method, parser type,

31
00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.611
or owned result can express the same
intent while preserving Rust's safe buffer

32
00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:18.020
model.
